<section id="experience-2401">
    <div class="cs-container">
        <!-- Experience -->
        <div class="cs-intro-wrapper">
            <div class="cs-section-header">
                <span class="cs-accent-line"></span>
                <h3 class="cs-section-title">Experience</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="cs-intro">
                I love the energy of starting something new. I also love the challenge of bringing order out of chaos. Things I don't love: resumés, LinkedIn, "tell us what value you bring to the table" — although I've dealt with them all. I've worked across multiple industries and positions, eventually being given opportunities to occupy nonprofit management roles and directorships. Each experience demanded the ability to learn new systems, responsibilities, and architect solutions. It's been said that my generation's greatest strength in the workplace is the ability to pivot and figure things out, and I agree with that assessment. At the end of the day, the ability to synthesize information, navigate unfamiliar territory, and turn information into action are among the most valuable capabilities to have in just about any situation.<br>
            </p>
            <p class="cs-intro">
                Some of the environments and areas I've worked in: public libraries, the Head Start program, housing management, affordable housing, financial literacy, freelancing, tech and web development, professional editing, proofreading and transcription, real estate, sales, service, teaching, compliance, finance, community organizing. Conventional career advice says to specialize, stay in one lane, and build upward. Like many of my peers, my path has looked a little different. The ability to integrate skills and knowledge from seemingly unrelated roles is precisely what has shaped the professional I am today.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Education -->
        <div class="cs-education-wrapper">
            <div class="cs-section-header">
                <span class="cs-accent-line"></span>
                <h3 class="cs-section-title">Education</h3>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-education-group">
            <p class="cs-intro">
                Like my career experiences, my educational journey has been characterized by exploration and integration. As a first-generation college student, I started out at community college, earning an associate of science in social science with a focus in psychology. I then completed coursework at Burlington College before continuing to the California Institute of Integral Studies, where I earned a bachelor of arts in interdisciplinary studies. CIIS was a particularly good fit: my curriculum brought together subjects including self and society, culture and community, integral learning, knowledge and inquiry, research and writing, global studies, and social change. I also completed graduate-level coursework in transpersonal psychology and leadership, including an introduction to systems thinking. Rather than following a single academic discipline, I learned to look for connections between ideas, examine systems and context, and approach questions from multiple perspectives.
            </p>
            <p class="cs-intro">
                Independent research and inquiry have always been a significant part of how I operate. I'm naturally inclined to pull at a thread until I understand where it leads — to investigate, identify patterns, seek out perspectives, and keep digging until I understand not just <em>what</em> is happening, but <em>why</em>. That tendency has served me well both inside and outside of academia. Some of my education has happened through business and marketing classes, mediation training, real estate licensing, coaching certifications, professional and personal development, and a great deal of self-directed study. The credentials are part of the story, but the more enduring education has been the habit of remaining curious and pursuing answers to what I don't yet understand.
            </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<script>
(() => {
    const observerOptions = {
        threshold: 0.15,
        rootMargin: '0px'
    };

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
        entries.forEach(entry => {
            if (entry.isIntersecting) {
                entry.target.classList.add('cs-visible');
                observer.unobserve(entry.target);
            }
        });
    }, observerOptions);

    const featured = document.querySelectorAll('#experience-2401 .cs-featured');
    featured.forEach(item => observer.observe(item));
})();
</script>

